# [HACKATHON](../wg/hackathon.html)

## Summary

The Hackathon project presentation session was chaired by Benno Overeinder. Participants presented two-minute summaries of work accomplished during the hackathon across a wide range of IETF-related projects, including Post-Quantum Cryptography (PQC) certificates, AI agent security and observability, YANG translations and normalization, DNSSEC enhancements, SRv6 service function chaining, L4S congestion control, and low-power wireless networking.

## Key Discussion Points

* **PQC Certificates**: John Gray presented updates on behalf of Aman regarding PQC certificate artifact testing. A PKI linter (developed by Corey Bonnell) was added to check submitted certificates from ~33 providers and flag linting issues. The project processed 22 pull requests and updated verifiers (including OQS and Carl Wallace's verifier).
* **StratoWeave (IETF YANG Translation)**: Demonstrated translation from IETF YANG models to vendor-specific YANG models (Cisco XR, Junos, Arista, Huawei VRP) to configure MPLS L3VPN services over NETCONF. Follow-up presentations planned in OPSAWG and NMOP.
* **Agent Action Evidence & COSE Verification**: Steven Mee, Laurent Lemanier, and Anton Sokolov tested cross-verification of agent action receipts across independent transparency services (12 cross-draft joins, 22 bad cases caught). Independent verification of COSE deterministic encodings achieved 33/33 pass rate, while identifying gaps in hash input rules in RFC 9942 and RFC 9943.
* **NetOps Agent Interoperability**: Showcased agent-to-agent interoperability using A2A and MCP protocols across multi-vendor environments. Demonstrated a master agent delegating traffic engineering/steering topology calculations to specialized agents.
* **CoAP over BPv7**: Remote presentation on running CoAP over Bundle Protocol version 7 (BPv7) across rover, satellite, and Earth nodes using 24-bit message IDs and 3-component IPN addressing (RFC 9758). Aggregating multiple CoAP requests into a single BPv7 bundle saved ~40 bytes.
* **Early Attestation Protocols**: Muhammad Usama Sardar presented formal analysis work (with Songbu) on early attestation read/choose protocols (RFC 9261) for health data exchanges. Identified critical vulnerabilities and reported end-of-life or unmaintained implementations in early attestation libraries.
* **AI Incident Diagnosis Agent**: Evaluated using LLM agents for network fault diagnosis. Highlighted that Knowledge Graphs are required to ground and guardrail LLM outputs against dynamic network corner cases.
* **SRv6-SFC Architecture**: Implemented BizFox (SRv6 SFC Orchestrator using intent fulfillment per RFC 9315) and TEAM (Flow-SRv6 Binder for Linux) to manage SRv6 service function chaining with SR-aware functions.
* **Batch Authorization Delegation**: Yuan Ren presented a framework where an orchestrator agent requests permissions for multiple specialized agents (e.g., calendar, email) in a single batch token using authorization details with `act`/`sub` fields.
* **ICON (Agent Observability & Control)**: Tested continuous monitoring and intervention for AI agents, implementing runtime metrics, failure attribution, and human operator intervention mechanisms (e.g., during 4K streaming session handovers).
* **Distributed Policy Sharing Model**: Lucia Cabanillas presented a YANG model for policy artifacts carrying metadata and provenance signatures, demonstrating cross-domain policy enforcement between Open Policy Agent (REGO) and an IPSec controller.
* **MIMI Interop**: Mark reported successful interoperability testing between two independent MIMI implementations (MimiD and Haven). Identified gaps in provider mutual authentication and draft assumptions, leading to draft updates.
* **Shared Open Verification & RFC 8785 JCS**: Sankalp tested 23 AI agent record projects with a shared open checker, prompting an immediate errata fix by one maintainer. Evaluated RFC 8785 (JSON Canonicalization Scheme) implementations across Rust, Go, Python, and JavaScript using over 20,000 test cases, identifying edge-case discrepancies (duplicate keys, large integers, deep nesting, BOM markers).
* **YANG Normalization**: Addressed YANG provenance signature invalidation caused by differing encodings (XML, JSON, CBOR). Built a prototype parsing encodings into a common AST to produce deterministic representations.
* **ILNP Full Stack Testing**: Salim, Rod Grimes, and Alastair Woodman demonstrated Identifier-Locator Network Protocol (RFC 6740–RFC 6748) end-to-end video streaming over IPv6 without using standard IP addresses, including seamless multi-homing flow migration across four interfaces.
* **GREEN WG Energy Model**: Tested device-level YANG energy models with a simulator, highlighting the central role of attribute relationships to avoid double counting and the need for new lifecycle states (e.g., restart).
* **IOAM Direct Export On-Path Delay**: Alex presented a VPP proof-of-concept extending IOAM Direct Export to compute on-path delay by inserting encapsulation timestamps and exporting via IPFIX.
* **Post-Quantum DNSSEC & MTL**: Joe presented testing of Merkle Tree Ladder (MTL) signatures with ML-DSA and SLH-DSA for DNSSEC. MTL reduced zone signing time by ~25% and reduced response sizes through signature deduplication and SIG tag extensions.
* **SRv6 TE over RSVP**: Andy Smith (with HPE and Juniper) demonstrated RSVP-TE over an SRv6 data plane without MPLS, achieving distributed admission control and bandwidth management across ArcOS and Junos routers.
* **Agentic Resource Discovery (ARD)**: Junjie presented discovery envelopes and APIs for AI agent resources (MCP tools, skills, agents) with multi-domain signature verification.
* **SRv6 SFC Control Plane**: Implemented BGP Flowspec SRv6 extensions on the vUFX software router for explicit SR policy forwarding.
* **iPerf2 L4S Support**: Bob McMahon announced L4S support in iPerf2 and the release of an Android app for capacity and latency measurement.
* **DNS Table Projects**: Stuart Cheshire summarized DNS project achievements:
  * ML-DSA-44 post-quantum signature support in `jdnssec-tools` (David).
  * TLS client authentication via DANE TLSA records draft prototype (Stuart Cheshire).
  * Generalized ZoneMD support in ISC BIND for root zone requirements (Mark Andrews, Evan Hunt).
  * Opportunistic Operator-Led Transport Signaling (OOTS) for authority servers (Johan Stenstam, Peter DeVries, Vicki Streste).
* **OpenA2A Agent Trust**: Abdel Feng and Alexander Perlov demonstrated AEP verifiers and WIMSE integration to counter prompt injection and verify agent identity and actions.
* **DotBots LAKE Protocol**: Giovanni tested the LAKE (EDHOC) key exchange protocol across 1,000 swarm robots communicating with a server over time-synchronized channel hopping networks, achieving average handshake times under two seconds.
* **Originator Profile (OP)**: Shijia Suzuki presented email and web page attribute validation bound to S/MIME keys, testing DNSSEC-based key rollover and revocation signals.
* **YANG Push to Message Broker**: Integrated YANG push telemetry with message brokers, extending `libyang` to support `anydata` validation (e.g., YANG Patch) using schema anchors.
* **QCBR CBOR Serialization**: QCBR v2 library implementation updating CBOR deterministic serialization rules based on the latest working group specification.
* **Agent AI Security Framework for Mobile Networks**: Joseph Anne presented closed-loop security policy management using multi-agent AI to maintain security policy continuity during handovers.
* **Agent AI Framework (AAIAF)**: Presented edge-agent orchestration of worker agents to dynamically adapt to intent changes and task obstacles.
* **SCHC Fragmentation over VXLAN & WIMSE**: Alexander Perlov demonstrated SCHC fragmentation to support VXLAN over low MTU links (500 Mbps achieved in C/Rust) and tested 9-hop agent-to-agent identity verification using WIMSE/OAuth tokens.
* **Open-Source ASN.1 Tool**: Clint presented a tool started by Corey Bonnell that extracts ASN.1 modules from drafts, automatically applies errata via Claude scripts, and decodes PEM artifacts.
* **Global Opaque Block (GOB) for IOAM**: Presented a tail-option extension for IOAM to carry path-level metrics (e.g., accumulated delay) using eBPF hooks in Linux.
* **Thread Low-Power Mesh Printing**: Demonstrated rendering images on a battery-powered E-ink screen via AirPrint/IPP over a Thread low-power IPv6 mesh network.
* **L4S Interop**: Greg White detailed testing of L4S congestion control senders (Netflix, Nvidia, Chrome WebRTC, Pion UDP prod, Screen v2 / Jitsi) sharing bottleneck links with dynamic capacity adaptation.
* **IoT Status & Lifecycle Reporting**: Evaluated status matching and attestation consistency between IoT edge devices and management platforms during firmware updates.

## Decisions and Action Items

* **Spec/Errata Fixes**: Multiple projects identified text discrepancies or bugs during interop (e.g., AER maintainer published errata based on open checker findings; ASN.1 tool identified errata in existing RFCs to be filed).
* **Draft Updates**: Pull requests and draft updates are planned based on hackathon findings for MIMI interop, COSE deterministic encodings, IOAM GOB, and YANG normalization (`OPSAWG`).

